{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request e1035ed7, identifiers (c+d), on proposal 4a3faa17.\nThree decisions. The second corrects item 3 of my ruling 913859be.\n\n1. The retained use: yes, as a separate arm with its own type.\n   A retained use is a statement of the local sequence, not a check of\n   the other one. It says that an entry of this sequence used a named\n   fact of a recorded kind, with a body of a recorded digest, under a\n   named producer. Ruling b02334db put the kind in the entry so that a\n   later reader needs no birth. This arm is that reader.\n   a. Its one constructor takes a local entry that the scope read from\n      its own store through `Own`, a FactUse that is in that entry, and\n      retained bytes whose digest equals the content of that use. The\n      fact, the kind and the producer come from the entry. A caller\n      supplies none of them.\n   b. It carries no CheckedSequence. It is never a Fetched, a Source,\n      `from.pin` evidence or an input to a Native or capability\n      projection that requires a birth.\n   c. `retainedFacts`, `Operations.#record`, `ReceiverReads.retained`,\n      the delivery result path, `Turns.#seal`, `applyEntry` and the\n      outbox diagnosis take this arm by name. Each reads the fact, the\n      kind, the body and the producer.\n   d. A consumer that needs a birth and holds only a retained use\n      gives its existing unavailable or incomplete answer. Tell me\n      which consumers those are, with the witness of each. Today's\n      retained value holds no birth, so I expect none.\n   e. Replay judges a retained use by its recorded entry, as 8894ef60\n      says. No remote birth is retained, no row is added and nothing\n      is fetched after a restart.\n\n2. The owner hook: none. No commit generation, no hook on `Turns`, no\n   notification from the archive or rebuild paths.\n   Why. A fact that a group reads is a sealed entry of another\n   sequence. Its bytes, its birth and its kind do not depend on the\n   local head. At main the four sites fetch before the turn, and the\n   judge reads local state inside `turns.run`, at the head of the\n   commit. A changed head between the read and the commit is already\n   judged there. The cutover does not change that order. My words\n   \"current identity\" and \"stale owner\" in 913859be asked for a guard\n   that no decision needs. They are withdrawn.\n   What stays from 913859be item 3.\n   a. `fetchFacts` takes one monotonic deadline when the group starts.\n      Each read gets the remaining milliseconds on its existing\n      `turnDeadline`, and `{maxBytes, milliseconds}` on the one\n      `Peer.source` call.\n   b. A group publishes nothing after its deadline. The group already\n      returns once, so a late reply has no place to arrive. The note\n      says that a logical timeout does not cancel a physical call.\n   c. Every direct caller of the resolver passes `{maxBytes,\n      milliseconds}` as required arguments, from its existing bound and\n      its existing timeout: the sender read in delivery, the Chains\n      callback, `Operations.#publicationReady`, the two snapshot\n      readers in worker.ts and testing.ts. No default and no optional\n      context.\n   If you find a value that a site reads before the group, uses to\n   admit a Foreign, and does not read again in the turn, stop and name\n   it. A hazard that main has today is a finding for a later head. It\n   is not this head's to close.\n\n3. `Own.sequence`: approved as proposed. `pinned()` admits the bytes\n   of `stored(0)` that it already reads and keeps the genuine token\n   beside the pinned data. `ownOf` takes the token as a required\n   argument, and every producer and wrapper of `Own` forwards it. The\n   append-before-fold case uses the sealed genesis hash and the address\n   from the seed. A missing or invalid birth stops the judgment. No\n   read, no body cache and no map is added.\n\nCap A, the reason word `source-response-too-large` and the work bound\nstand as the proposal states them.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}